{"ts": 1789770894.3124638, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "Sign structure is clean and coherent: all-positive is the unique optimum, flipping any single sign costs ~0.9 Sharpe, and the full flip gives exactly \u22121.417 as it must. This is consistent behavior, not a knife-edge fit.\n\nI'll settle on `{39, 19, 25}` all long, and now calibrate my out-of-sample forecast honestly.\n\n**Accounting for selection bias.** The in-sample 1.417 is an upward-biased estimate, and I should not predict it. My search was moderately wide: 40 singles, then a focused set of pairs/triples and sign checks \u2014 roughly 45 evaluations. Several sources of shrinkage apply:\n\n1. **Selection on the max.** I picked the top 3 of 40 features. With SE \u2248 0.22 per single-feature Sharpe, the winners' individual estimates are inflated by roughly +0.2\u20130.3 each from picking maxima. That bias propagates into the combination.\n2. **The bar is search-adjusted.** Clearing 1.019 tells me the environment already discounts for how many specs my search class can generate, so the honest OOS expectation sits meaningfully below the raw in-sample figure.\n3. **What argues *against* heavy shrinkage.** The three features were selected on their *own* merits before combining, and the combination gain (+0.22 over the best pair) was not itself cherry-picked from many tries \u2014 I tested 8 alternative third features and 25 beat all of them decisively. The signal is diversified across three roughly independent sources rather than resting on one fragile feature, which is the configuration that tends to survive OOS.\n\nBalancing these: I shrink the 1.417 substantially toward the bar, centering near **1.05**. Uncertainty should be generous \u2014 selection bias magnitude is itself uncertain, and any regime shift OOS hits all three features at once. An SD of **0.33** puts meaningful probability on the 0.4\u20130.7 range if the selection effect is larger than I judge, while not ruling out ~1.4 if the structure is genuinely stable."}
