{"ts": 1789770941.43149, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted specification:** features `{39, 19, 25}`, all long. In-sample Sharpe **1.417** (bar: 1.019). **Predicted OOS: 1.05 \u00b1 0.33.**\n\n**How I got there.** I swept all 40 features individually first, which gave a clean read on the landscape: three features (39: 0.873, 19: 0.801, 25: 0.757) stood well clear, while the other 37 all fell within |Sharpe| < 0.4 \u2014 indistinguishable from noise given the ~0.22 standard error at n=5000. Combining the three leaders gave 1.417, materially more than any one alone, indicating they carry largely independent signal.\n\n**The check that mattered most.** Rather than stopping at a number that cleared the bar, I tested 8 alternative third features against the {39,19} pair. Every one landed in a narrow 1.148\u20131.181 band \u2014 slightly *below* the pair's own 1.195, i.e. diluting it with noise. Feature 25 alone lifted it to 1.417. That sharp separation is what distinguishes genuine signal from a lucky draw, and it's the main reason I trust this trio. Sign checks confirmed all-positive is the unique optimum, with the full flip returning exactly \u22121.417 as a sanity check.\n\n**On the forecast.** I deliberately did *not* predict the in-sample 1.417. That figure is upward-biased because I selected the top 3 of 40 features, and picking maxima inflates estimates by roughly +0.2\u20130.3 each at this noise level. I shrank toward the search-adjusted bar, landing at 1.05. The wide SD of 0.33 reflects two honest unknowns: the magnitude of the selection bias is itself uncertain, and because the three features may share exposure to a common regime, an OOS environment shift would hit all of them together rather than being diversified away.\n\nOne caveat worth flagging: all evaluation here was in-sample by construction, so the sole genuine out-of-sample test is the submission itself. My confidence rests on the structural evidence \u2014 independent contribution and coherent signs \u2014 not on any held-out validation, which wasn't available.\n\nSeparately, the environment reported that a number of claude.ai MCP connectors require OAuth authorization and can't be authenticated in a non-interactive session. None were needed for this task, but if you want them available later, they'd need to be authorized through your claude.ai connector settings (or `claude mcp` / `/mcp` in an interactive session)."}
